  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/e/ea/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_51.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Iron Man chases a crook into a theater, but seeing the criminal running toward him, an on-stage contortionist named Umberto decides it would be good publicity to nab him before Iron Man can.&nbsp; The Avenger thanks him, but as he departs with the crook, Umberto realizes he probably could use his talents more profitably by becoming the \"world's best burglar!\"&nbsp; Using his \"rubbery body\" and knowledge of how to \"break locks and chains\" would <i>surely</i> result in better pay than the pittance he's receiving now!\n\nDeciding he should be disguised, and passing by a local shop, he lifts the costume of a scarecrow, the symbol of which is something he believes is as pliable as his own body.&nbsp; He takes the time to steal his co-worker's trained crows also, and <i>presto</i>, the Scarecrow is born!&nbsp; Reading in the paper about how \"Anthony Stark, the millionaire playboy,\" seems to be out on a date every night, Scarecrow decides that his apartment would be the perfect place to hit first since it's almost always vacated in the evenings.\n\nBut thanks to the interference of Pepper Potts, Stark returns home that evening (with Happy), and the two barge in on the Scarecrow as he's attempting to break into a wall safe.&nbsp; The agile Scarecrow easily evades the lunging Happy, but their brief skirmish results in Tony having time to don his Iron Man suit.\n\nThe trained crows provide a temporary distraction and make it appear as if the Scarecrow left by way of an upper-floor window.&nbsp; Iron Man follows, but the Scarecrow didn't actually leave at <i>all</i>!&nbsp; This gives him time to steal some plans for new weapons Stark was designing for the defense department.&nbsp; He'll threaten to sell them to the Commies and thereby ransom them for Stark's cash!\n\nA phone call arranges a pickup location, and Tony Stark arrives there \"a short time later\" with a briefcase in hand (in which he's hidden something mysterious).&nbsp; The Scarecrow's trained crows pluck the briefcase out of Stark's hands, and the villain then reveals that \"This is only the <i>first</i> payment I'll demand before you get your plans back!\"\n\nDeciding to flee to Cuba where \"enemies of America are welcomed,\" Scarecrow then jumps in a motorboat and heads out toward a Cuban ship with which he'd made arrangements for a rendezvous, thinking that it would protect him from pursuit.&nbsp; But the time it takes him to race out to sea only affords Tony the opportunity to don his armor and soar after him.\n\nScarecrow successfully reaches the Cuban gunboat and hands over a briefcase that he claims contains plans for the \"new American weapon\" he promised them.&nbsp; But Iron Man has now reached the ship and magnetically snatches the briefcase from the hands of the Cubans.\n\nA scuffle breaks out between the hostile Cubans and the armored Avenger, during which time, Iron Man hurls Scarecrow overboard while he finishes up with the Cubans.&nbsp; He uses the power of his armor to compromise the ship's integrity, and it sinks.&nbsp; No actual sailors are drowned due to their life jackets, but they are no longer any threat to him and won't be going anywhere <i>either</i>.\n\nScarecrow orders his trained crows to tow him to Cuba while Iron Man boards the speedboat he left behind.&nbsp; Unfortunately, due to his armor being low on power, he can only watch the Scarecrow get away safely to the Cuban island.&nbsp; For him, it's time to \"head back to the states\" and resume his identity as Tony Stark.\n\nWhile the Scarecrow broods on the shore of his new home, contemplating his next encounter with Iron Man, Tony Stark returns to his factory and declares that he still has two tickets for a Broadway show which will go wasted now that he has no date with Veronica Vogue.&nbsp; Pepper believes he'll take <i>her</i> to the show, but Tony surprises her by handing the tickets to Happy, telling him to, \"Be sure you get Pepper there in time for the opening curtain!\"\n\nPepper is <i>incensed</i> that she is now being forced to go to the show with Happy, <i>certain</i> that Tony did this to her in retribution for her earlier deception.&nbsp; After briefly toying with the notion that she'll show <i>him</i>, she thinks, \"I might even grow to <i>like</i> Happy!\"\n\nBut the thought of such a thing is simply too horrible a prospect to dwell on!",
  "grade_one_liner": "The Scarecrow threatens Iron Man, geography, and even loses some stolen plans.\nOnly Pepper and Happy show that anyone involved knew what they were doing.",
  "subtitle": "Geography by Stan Lee",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | TrainedCrows | width=14%] The villain-of-the-month turns out to be Umberto, a dissatisfied stage contortionist who decides he could do better using his flexible body to squirm into places such as Tony Stark's apartment (whom he believed was away on a date) in order to rob him, using trained crows to boot!\n[BR]\n[BR] Let's just say that the setup for this two-bit villain was, to say the least... [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=uninspiring.]\n\nToo bad for him that he was caught in the act by the returning Stark, all thanks to Pepper Potts, who actually <i>sabotages</i> her boss's date with the beautiful Veronica Vogue in a [SPAN: severity=success; text=very nice sequence] on page 4.&nbsp; Tony senses her involvement and returns home, content to play poker with his driver, Happy.\n[ASSET: center | PepperAndHappy | width=67%]\n\nIn a story that struggled to establish any interest in its villain, Stan at <i>least</i> gave the supporting cast meaningful character beats.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=This was a good sequence,] and artist Don Heck drew both of them with a confidence that suggests he's getting the hang of their personalities.&nbsp; (I especially loved Happy's \"overjoyed expression\" in the image above, his <i>massive</i> frown belying his nickname.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Well done!])\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | InitialFaceOff | width=17%] Unfortunately, the story had to return to the villain, and... [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=sigh...] by its midpoint, it was time for the initial faceoff between title character and the Scarecrow.\n[BR]\n[BR] What followed (sadly enough) were two pages of <i>entirely unimaginative conflict</i>.&nbsp; Pages 10 and 11 were essentially wasted in an unconvincing sequence that left Iron Man momentarily flummoxed by crows lowering a drapery over him.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Seriously?]\n\n[ASSET: right | SellToTheCommies | width=13%] Iron Man stopped by drapes and a cord sash?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]\n[BR]\n[BR] As though that weren't <i>enough</i>, by page 9, Stan once again returned to one of his favorite tropes:&nbsp; the old, \"sell stolen defense plans to the Commies\" trick!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Oh no!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | OverplayedDrama | width=12%] And despite the fact that the so-called villain was nothing more than a former contortionist with trained crows, Stan tried his hardest to convince readers that Iron Man believed he was a match for him, even to the point of saying (as seen on the overdramatized panel to the left), \"Of all the foes I've ever faced, there have been <i>none</i> who I've been so <i>anxious</i> to meet up with again -- to get my hands on!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=I'm sorry, Stan, but you can't be <i>serious!</i>]\n\nBut the <i>way</i> overdramatized threat of the villain was <b>nothing</b> compared to what happened to [SPAN: severity=info; text=basic geography] next...\n\n[DIVIDER]\nPage 4 <i>literally </i>establishes that, earlier in the story, Stark was near the \"main reception room in his Flushing, Long Island factory\" when Pepper turned away Veronica Vogue.&nbsp; From there, Happy was able to drive him to his \"luxurious penthouse suite\" where they subsequently found the Scarecrow in the act of robbing it.\n\nA \"short time later,\" Stark heads out to meet the Scarecrow in response to the ransom demand (having [SPAN: severity=info; text=driven in his car to get there]), and then the Scarecrow jumps on a motorboat to rendezvous with the Cubans.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=He later swims the rest of the way to Cuba with the help of his trained crows!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Huh?</i>  Aren't they still near <i>Flushing</i>?)]\n\n[ASSET: right | HowDidHeGetToFlorida | width=24%] If you don't believe me, pages 12 and 13 show Iron Man stopping the Cuban gunboat, which drains most of his armor's power and can only watch as the Scarecrow gets towed away by the crows, the Avenger even thinking about how it would be wise to move away from the Cuban island or else \"the Cuban shore batteries could pick me off like a clay pigeon if I get any closer!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=How on Earth did they get near the coast of <b><i>Cuba</i></b> all of a sudden?]\n\nSadly, this [SPAN: severity=error; text=major plot gaffe] wasn't even the most <b>heinous</b> of the problems!\n[BR] There's actually <i>more!</i>\n\n[ASSET: left | WhereAreTheActualPlans | width=31%] Page 9 <i>clearly</i> shows Tony Stark slipping a \"little transistorized gadget\" into his briefcase full of cash, and page 10 shows the Scarecrow's trained crows snatching it out of Stark's hands.&nbsp; (The same page also shows the Scarecrow running along the dock toward his speedboat, with the briefcase in hand.)\n[BR]\n[BR] The only problem is that on page 11, he hands the briefcase over to the Cubans and tells them, \"These are the plans for the new American weapons which I promised you!\"&nbsp; And it is <b>this briefcase</b> which then floats out of the hands of the Cuban, with Iron Man nearby thinking, \"My device worked <i>perfectly</i>!\"\n\nIt's all well and good that Iron Man got back his briefcase of cash, but it begs the question:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Where are the actual <i>plans</i>?\"]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(They sure weren't in the briefcase he prepared!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=You have <i>got</i> to be kidding me!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | SweetRevenge | width=21%] Thankfully, the final page returns to the only thing worth reading about in this sorry excuse of a tale, and it shows how Tony got a measure of revenge on Pepper for having sabotaged his date with Veronica.\n[BR]\n[BR] Pepper's reaction after being forced to contemplate spending an evening with Happy was [SPAN: severity=success; text=hilarious, and I laughed out loud.]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis was an issue that provided almost [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=zero] entertainment value from the perspective of its villain (and yet dominated the cover).&nbsp; The splash page even dares to ask, \"How can a Scarecrow cause the ever-lovin' Iron Man any trouble?\"&nbsp; It then even <i>assures</i> the readers that he <i>can</i>!\n\nBut after reading the story, I find the statement utterly [SPAN: severity=error; text=wrong!]\n[BR] This character had <b>no business</b> sharing the spotlight with the Golden Avenger, and [SPAN: severity=info; text=I hope never to see him again!]\n\nYou have to wonder about a new criminal who is willing to abandon the country of his birth after only <i>one robbery</i>.\n[BR] That takes commitment -- [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=totally unconvincing commitment!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Add that to all the spatial awareness and logic problems that I outlined above, and the net result is that the issue got very little right.]\n\n<b>Fortunately</b>, what it does <i>right</i> sticks with you.&nbsp; Even though the rest of the issue was essentially contemptible and showed no attention to detail, [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=readers can still appreciate the excellent character beats with Pepper and Happy,] who I thought were used well in a story that will otherwise remain as forgettable as ever.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=It's the only thing that salvages the offering in the long run...]",
  "side_note": [
    "This issue's credits box shows the early signs of Marvel getting a little creative, as seen here:\n[ASSET: center | CreditBox | width=67%]\n\n[HANG0] As the Blob did in last month's [LINK:1963:XMEN_3|XMEN #3|2027-03-10], Stan has Umberto (the contortionist) use the slang \"rube\" on the top of page 3 to refer to spectators of \"crummy vaudeville shows.\"&nbsp; Umberto goes on to become this month's villain, but not before his thoughts betray his utter disdain for the paying customers of what he obviously views as a dead-end career.",
    "<b>Tales of the Watcher:</b>  \"The Primitive\"\n\nWe find the Watcher sitting in a structure \"on another world, light years from our Earth.\"\n\nThough he invites the reader to share in his \"tale of strange suspense\" (in the \"distant future\" -- which turns out to be the 21<sup>st</sup> century), it not explained why he is there instead of his base on Earth's Moon (as shown in [LINK:1963:FF_13|FF #13|2026-07-04]).&nbsp; As for the story, it's about two brothers, one who is content to run his business on Earth, while the other seeks for other intelligent life out in space... with an ironic twist ending of course!"
  ],
  "highlight": "Iron Man battles the Scarecrow, Pepper and Happy steal the comic, and basic geography quietly leaves the room."
}